  "statement": "Mandated third-party model evaluations are administrative tools, not moral shields, and will either impose performative compliance that entrenches dominant firms or provide rigorous safety checks depending entirely on how liability and cost are allocated. The Commerce Department and NIST face a structural choice under upcoming Office of Management and Budget guidance for federal procurement: whether to institute enforceable, performance-gated independent audits tied to concrete technical thresholds, or to permit voluntary standards that leave safety oversight to marketing departments. Frontier AI developers and commercial testing labs currently operate under financial incentives that make voluntary disclosures toothless, proving that without statutory teeth, external evaluations function as public relations exercises rather than risk controls. To prevent corporate capture, any federal evaluation regime must target specific computational thresholds, such as training runs exceeding 10 to the 26th integer operations, rather than applying blunt mandates that bankrupt open-source innovators while leaving hyperscalers untouched.\n\n Critics rightly argue that rigid NIST mandates and third-party evaluations can impose insurmountable compliance barriers that crush open-source innovation and hand the market entirely to a handful of well-resourced corporations. This critique holds weight because compliance costs in software auditing disproportionately affect smaller teams who lack the legal and technical staff to navigate federal certification bureaucracies. However, exempting smaller developers entirely from safety scrutiny creates a regulatory loophole where dangerous models migrate to low-oversight jurisdictions or get deployed through unregulated application programming interfaces. The solution is not abandoning evaluations, but structuring them with tiered performance gates that exempt models below specific capability floors while requiring verifiable audit trails only for systems deployed at critical national security scale. This balance protects open-source developers from compliance bankruptcy while ensuring that models deployed in federal procurement channels meet verifiable safety baselines.\n\n The enforcement mechanism rests within the Federal Acquisition Regulation, where the Commerce Department can deny procurement contracts to developers who refuse independent third-party audits. This creates a direct commercial penalty for non-compliance without requiring a sprawling new federal police force that would invite immediate legislative gridlock. Historical precedent from cybersecurity compliance regimes, such as the Federal Risk and Authorization Management Program, demonstrates that procurement leverage forces compliance faster than civil penalties alone. The coalition supporting this approach includes procurement officers seeking liability protection and mid-sized software firms locked out of current enterprise markets, while the opposing coalition comprises dominant tech incumbents seeking to amortize compliance costs they can easily absorb. Policymakers must decide whether federal procurement rules will reward actual safety verification or simply certify whichever firm can afford the highest auditing fees.",
